# Copy to .env and fill in. `.env` is git-ignored; this file is not, so nothing here may be a
# real value.
#
# What is enforced, and where. Compose checks exactly one thing: it refuses to interpolate this
# file at all while `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` is unset. Every other required value is enforced by the
# process that reads it, naming the one it wanted — ingestion will not start without its three,
# the backend without the shared secret, and the bot without the backend's address, that same
# secret, its own platform token and the list of ids it will answer. Enforced in the process
# rather than by Compose, because an interpolation Compose refuses stops every command for every
# service, so one unset value would read as a stack nobody can inspect. The deploy script checks
# the ones it can before it builds anything, so an unset value stops a deploy instead of leaving
# a container restarting behind one that reported success.
#
# Blank counts as unset: Compose writes an unset variable through as an empty string, and an
# empty credential reaching a platform comes back as a refusal that reads like the source being
# unreachable.
#
# The rest default to empty and the stack starts without them, so an otherwise incomplete file
# does not announce itself — which is why the deploy script looks at the enforced ones rather
# than leaving a container to restart.

# --- store ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_DB=mainbrain
POSTGRES_USER=mainbrain
# Required. Compose refuses to start without it rather than defaulting to something guessable.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# --- embeddings sidecar -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Both have working defaults in Compose and are here to be read rather than set.
#
# The weights are gigabytes, fetched from the model's host into a volume while the service
# starts rather than when the first request arrives, and the sidecar reports itself ready only
# once it can answer. A first start on a machine that has never run it is therefore slow and
# needs the network; every start after that reads the volume.
#
# The model is not a free choice: the index is declared at its dense width and its vocabulary
# size, so another model is a migration and a re-index rather than a restart. Leaving this
# unset in the environment takes the default, which is the model the schema matches; setting
# it to something else without migrating gives a sidecar the store will refuse to accept
# vectors from.
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=intfloat/multilingual-e5-base
#
# A model of the same width is a swap; one of another width is a migration. Several open
# models share this one's width, so trying another of them needs no schema change — only a
# re-index, which the version string the sidecar reports brings about on its own.

# --- model ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Belongs to the backend alone — recognition and the answer path are the only callers. The
# ingest container must never receive it: it holds live session credentials, and capture
# needs no model access.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#
# TWO MODELS, because the two kinds of work are opposites. Answering is a dozen calls a day
# whose quality a person reads; the bulk passes — recognition's inference, and the
# classification that routes free text — are thousands of calls producing mechanical output,
# and they are most of what this build costs. One credential and one connection serve both:
# the model travels on each request rather than on the connection.
#
# Which model answers a question. The dearer of the two, and the one worth it.
# ANSWER_MODEL=claude-opus-5
#
# And which does the bulk work. Priced at three fifths of the above per token either way.
#
# MOVING THIS NORMALLY RE-DERIVES THE HORIZON, and for the one move this build makes it does
# not. An extractor's watermark carries the model it read with, because a different model reads a
# window differently — so changing this ordinarily makes every window inside
# RECOGNITION_HORIZON_DAYS owed again, at the new model's prices, once. For the switch from the
# answer model to this one, `V13` rewrites the marks already recorded instead: that output was
# read and paid for, and re-reading it would replace findings rather than add any. Any further
# change does re-derive — narrow the horizon first, and read `V13`'s note on what it trades.
#
# Reaching further down the price list is not a matter of naming a cheaper model: every request
# this build sends carries adaptive thinking and an effort level, and the small-model family
# accepts neither. That would be an adapter change, and extraction quality below this tier is
# unmeasured — hedged language and relative dates are the first things expected to suffer.
# BULK_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# --- what one question may cost -----------------------------------------------------------
# EFFORT IS THE DIAL HERE, AND IT IS THE SAFE ONE: it trades reasoning depth against spend and
# nothing else. There is deliberately no variable for a single call's ceiling — thinking counts
# against that ceiling, so lowering one buys a truncation rather than a cheaper answer, and this
# build reports a truncation as having nothing to answer from. That failure is indistinguishable
# from a corpus that really holds no answer, which makes it the worst thing to configure by hand.
#
# How hard the four corpus-reading shapes and the free-form fallback think. These are handed what
# to read, so what is left is composing an answer out of material already in front of the model —
# work this model does well below the top of its range. One of low, medium, high, xhigh, max; a
# misspelling stops the process rather than quietly restoring the default.
# ANSWER_EFFORT=medium
#
# And the one shape that reads the open web, which is the only path where the model searches and
# reads inside the call. Kept high here and nowhere else, so that setting reaches one shape
# instead of five.
# RESEARCH_EFFORT=xhigh
#
# The ceiling on everything one question spends, across every call it makes and the one retry.
# This is the only thing standing between a pathological question and the provider's patience: a
# shape's own ceiling bounds one call, where a turn may pause and be resumed and a rejected reply
# is re-rolled once. The default is three full calls' worth. It cannot be set below the largest
# single call, because every call would then be sized to the remainder and truncate.
# ANSWER_MAX_TOKENS_PER_REQUEST=196608
#
# The zone the archive's correspondents write in, read by two services for two purposes.
#
# In the backend it is what each block of material is headed with when the model is shown it, so
# that a message saying "tomorrow" can be worked out into a day: that is done against the moment
# the message was written, and done in UTC it lands a day out for messages written in the hours
# where the local day and the UTC day disagree — just after midnight for a zone ahead of UTC, late
# evening for one behind. In the bot it is the calendar a citation's dates are named in, so the
# same message is dated the same way in an answer's prose and in the sources under it; the two
# read one variable because disagreeing would be one defect wearing two settings.
#
# Nothing can read this: an
# instant carries no zone, the platform never reports one, and inferring it from when people write
# was measured against a real corpus and pinned no single zone. So it is named here or it is UTC.
# A region name rather than an offset, because a corpus reaching back years is dated wrongly for
# half of it by a fixed offset wherever summer time is observed. A value naming no zone stops the
# process rather than quietly restoring UTC.
# CORPUS_TIME_ZONE=Europe/Lisbon

# --- the derived passes -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both run in the backend, on their own schedules, and every value here has a default the
# deployment does not need to state. A malformed one stops the process rather than reading as
# unset: taken leniently, a typo would silently restore the default, so the one value you could
# be sure was not in force would be the one you had set.
#
# How long to leave between sweeps of the indexing pass. The default is one minute, which is
# what decides how soon a captured message becomes retrievable. Indexing is local compute
# against the sidecar on this host, so there is no budget to protect by raising it.
# INDEXING_SECONDS_BETWEEN_SWEEPS=60
#
# And between sweeps of recognition. The default is an hour, because inference spends the model
# budget per window of conversation. A sweep drains whatever is owed when it starts, so this is
# how long a newly captured message waits to be read; what bounds how much recognition pays to read
# it is the pair of daily allowances below.
# RECOGNITION_SECONDS_BETWEEN_SWEEPS=3600
#
# The recency horizon: how far back inference reads, as a duration behind now. THIS, THE SWITCH
# BELOW IT, THE TWO CLOSURE BOUNDS AND THE TWO ALLOWANCES UNDER THEM ARE WHAT SPENDS MONEY ON THE
# SERVING STACK — the evaluation stack's own overrides at the end of this file spend on its
# fixture corpus and answer to that section: the switch decides whether a sweep spends anything at
# all, this decides how much
# work it may find, the closure bounds decide how often a promise is re-read and how long for,
# and the two allowances decide what a day pays towards all of it. This default is set by the budget
# rather than by what would answer best: inference is a model call per window of twenty messages
# plus one per finding and one per window to warm what those findings share, and a sweep drains
# until nothing is owed or the day stops it — so a wide horizon has everything inside it owed at
# once, and is paid off a day at a time. Fourteen days is a number the provisioned credit
# survives.
#
# Widen it deliberately, watching what a sweep reports it cost: every batch logs its token spend,
# and raising this is a deploy plus a re-derivation inside the horizon, which spends model tokens
# and touches no source. Overspending is the direction that does not reverse.
#
# Capture and indexing are deliberately NOT bounded by it and neither spends the credit, so the
# corpus and the index reach back as far as the archive allows whatever this says. Identity
# ignores it entirely.
# RECOGNITION_HORIZON_DAYS=14
#
# How many messages one sweep of inference selects. THE ONLY LEVER ON HOW FAST A BACKLOG DRAINS,
# and the cadence above is not one: a stage holds a single submission with the provider at a time, so
# the rate is this figure over a batch's turnaround — minutes, measured — and polling more often
# only shortens the wait before a finished batch is read.
#
# It paces rather than prices. A message is its own classification window and its own restatements
# however many it is read beside, so raising this moves when a day's reading happens rather than
# what that reading comes to; the allowances below are still what bound a day, and a sweep that
# runs into one says so and leaves the rest owed. Nor does it re-derive: how many messages were
# selected together is not part of the extractor's version, unlike how much conversation each of
# them was read inside.
#
# Raise it where a backlog is waiting on a deadline — a few hundred owed messages at the default is
# hours of sweeping — and leave it where nothing is, since a stack that has caught up has little
# owed per sweep whatever this says.
# RECOGNITION_MESSAGES_PER_SWEEP=16
#
# How far past a promise the closure stage keeps looking for the message that reports the thing as
# done. UNLIKE THE HORIZON ABOVE THIS IS MEASURED FROM EACH COMMITMENT, not from the sweep, so it
# bounds the re-reading rather than which commitments are eligible: a promise older than this is
# still examined once, which is what a backfilled archive needs, and what the bound stops is an
# unclosed promise being re-examined for as long as its conversation keeps growing. Once a
# conversation has been weighed this far past a promise, that commitment is settled whether or not
# anything closed it.
#
# What it trades is a promise discharged late — a quarter after it was made, say — against
# re-reading every conversation for this long after every promise in it. The stage spends against
# the same daily allowance as the reading stages below, one call per commitment over a bounded
# window of what followed it.
# RECOGNITION_CLOSURE_DAYS=30
#
# How much new material it takes to bring a commitment back for another examination, inside the
# CLOSURE horizon above rather than the recency one. The other half of the same judgement, and the
# half measured in messages rather than days: that horizon says how long a closure is worth
# looking for, this says how much has to have arrived before looking again is worth a model call.
#
# One call is billed for its instruction and the promise whether it weighs one new message or a
# full window of them, so with no floor a conversation arriving a message at a time buys a call
# per message and pays mostly for the fixed part.
#
# Raise it to spend less and find a closure later; lower it for the reverse, and at 1 the stage is
# back to re-examining for a single message. WHAT IT COSTS IS LATENCY AND NEVER A FINDING: the
# floor lapses as the closure horizon closes, so material a bounded window could not reach is read
# before the commitment is settled, however little of it there is. Nor can it leave a commitment
# unexamined — a commitment nothing has read, and one whose prompt or model moved, is examined
# whatever followed the promise.
# RECOGNITION_CLOSURE_REARM_MESSAGES=5
#
# WHETHER INFERENCE RUNS AT ALL. Off unless this is exactly `true`. The horizon above bounds how
# much a sweep reads; this decides whether a sweep reads anything, and it is off by default
# because the cost of being wrong only goes one way: a backend that is up sweeps on its own
# schedule, at a model call per window of conversation, and the first evidence of that is the
# balance rather than a log line somebody is watching.
#
# Identity is unaffected and still runs -- it is identifier matching and label reading, it costs
# nothing, and inference could not run ahead of it anyway. So a stack with this off still
# recognises contacts and clients; what it does not do is read commitments, events or attributes
# out of a conversation.
#
# Turn it on deliberately, having decided what the sweep is allowed to spend, and watch what it
# reports: every batch logs its token spend. Anything other than `true` or `false` stops the
# process rather than being read as one of them.
# RECOGNITION_INFERENCE_ENABLED=false
#
# What recognition may spend on the model in a calendar day, on each axis separately. These are
# the settings that bound an unattended night: the horizon above bounds how much work a sweep may
# FIND, and these bound how much recognition pays to read it. Unbounded, one run over a real
# backlog spends the whole provisioned credit — after which the next question asked of the bot
# comes back as a provider refusal about the balance, which reads to whoever asked as an
# unexplained failure.
#
# A DAY AND NOT A SWEEP. A sweep drains until nothing is owed, so the cadence says nothing about
# cost; and a per-sweep bound is granted afresh every cadence, so a night costs as many of them as
# it has hours. The day's total is read out of the store, which is also why restarting the backend
# grants no fresh allowance: a container started after an allowance is gone makes no model call at
# all, and the figure can be read without that container.
#
# TWO AXES, BECAUSE THE PROVIDER PRICES THEM AN ORDER OF MAGNITUDE APART. Output costs five times
# input. Either one running out stops the reading for the rest of that day, because a prompt change
# can move one a long way without touching the other. Both are counted as the provider reports
# them, along with what was read from and written to its cache; the cache counters are recorded and
# not bounded.
#
# HOW THESE ARE SIZED. A record costs about 3,300 input tokens and 900 output, measured over a full
# cycle against a real archive and already halved by the batch endpoint. So the defaults admit a
# backlog of a thousand-odd records draining over about three days, leave standing still at the
# rate new material arrives well inside them, and refuse a re-reading of the whole horizon after a
# prompt or model change — which costs more per record than a steady sweep and is the one event
# that can empty the credit in an afternoon. Raise them to backfill faster, watching what each
# sweep reports it cost.
#
# Records a stopped day did not reach are left owed and unmarked, and the log says which axis ran
# out and when the allowance refreshes — so a pause reads differently from a corpus with nothing in
# it, and differently from a stall.
#
# Only the reading spends: indexing embeds against the sidecar and identity matches identifiers in
# the store, so both keep running on a day whose allowance is gone. A question asked of the bot is
# answered too — the answer path is bounded per request and consults no day.
# RECOGNITION_INPUT_TOKENS_PER_DAY=1300000
# RECOGNITION_OUTPUT_TOKENS_PER_DAY=360000
#
# Whether the pass that judges which named conversations stand for a body of work runs at all, and
# how long between its sweeps.
#
# Identity reads a client off any shared conversation's name. That is right where every named
# conversation is work somebody is doing, and wrong where a source mixes client threads with
# thematic tracks -- referrals, partner programmes, banks, hiring, a shelf of resources -- because
# each of those carries a label exactly as a client's thread does and becomes a client too. This is
# the pass that tells them apart, by showing the model the name together with a sample of what is
# being said under it.
#
# OFF, for the reason inference is off and one more of its own. It spends, so a stack nobody meant
# to leave running must not start paying on its own schedule; and what it buys depends on the
# source -- where every named conversation is already a body of work, every call returns the answer
# identity had already read off the label and the tokens buy no change in output. Turn it on where
# the taxonomy mixes the two.
#
# NOTHING WAITS FOR IT. A conversation it has not judged is a client exactly as it was, so leaving
# this off is the corpus as the rest of the build reads it rather than a stage of the answer path
# missing. What it rejects is withheld where clients are read and never deleted: a wrong verdict is
# undone by rewording the instruction, which re-judges what the old wording decided.
#
# It draws on a fraction of the two daily allowances above rather than on the whole of them, so
# that a second spending pass adds a little to what a day can cost rather than doubling it;
# recognition keeps the figures as set. The fraction is in the code, because what it bounds is one
# pass against another and not a figure anybody sizes on its own. The cadence is the longest in
# this file because the pass converges -- once every conversation carries a verdict there is
# nothing to do until one arrives or the instruction moves. Every batch logs what it judged, what
# it rejected, and what it spent.
# ADMISSION_ENABLED=false
# ADMISSION_SECONDS_BETWEEN_SWEEPS=86400

# --- bot to backend ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret on the one hop between them. Both ends read it at startup and stop without it:
# the backend refuses every request that does not carry it, and the bot sends it on every
# question it asks. No development default, because a fallback value is a published value and
# the surface it guards answers questions about a whole archive. Any long random string will do:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
BACKEND_SECRET=

# --- the assistant chat -------------------------------------------------------------------
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# Comma-separated Telegram user identifiers. The allowlist is enforced at the edge: a message
# from anyone else has no work done on its behalf and gets no reply at all, and a group
# conversation is refused however familiar the sender. Required and refused empty — a bot that
# admits nobody ignores every message, which from outside reads exactly like one that is down.
# An entry that is not a number stops the process rather than quietly locking somebody out.
ALLOWED_TELEGRAM_IDS=

# --- ingestion ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Whether this deployment reads a source at all. On unless set to false, because continuous
# capture is what this system is: an installation never told about this backfills and then
# refreshes as before. A value that is neither true nor false stops the process.
#
# SET IT PER DEPLOY, NOT IN THIS FILE, when more than one stack shares one `.env`:
#
#   INGEST_ENABLED=false ./scripts/deploy.sh --env dev
#
# Compose prefers the shell over the file, so that switches off the one stack being brought up. A
# line written here reaches every stack that reads this file — including whichever one is actually
# ingesting, which would then stop reading and say so only in its own log. What that costs is the
# reading, not the corpus: a conversation's front edge is measured against the source on every
# start, so the stretch a quiet stack missed is read back when one starts again. What no restart
# repairs is a hole further back than that edge, which is what the re-read exists for.
#
# What it is for: a stack that is not the one ingesting. Every deployment pointed at the same
# session directory competes for one session, because the client library locks the directory it is
# given — see TELEGRAM_SESSION_HOST_PATH below, which decides whether that directory is shared.
# Pointed at a directory of its own instead, a second stack takes no lock and wants a sign-in of
# its own, which is a budget worth not spending. It also skips an unfinished backfill, and stops
# the index target rising under a deploy's drain wait; a backfill already finished is not re-walked
# either way, because the plan lives in the store.
#
# The service stays up either way and says in its log, and in the health row, that it is reading
# nothing — so a switched-off stack still passes the check that every container came up. The three
# values below are still required when it is off: this switches the reading, not what the process
# is configured with.
# INGEST_ENABLED=false
#
# A user-account application, from https://my.telegram.org/apps. Separate from the bot token:
# these are the two halves of Telegram's two different roles in this system.
#
# The session itself is NOT configured here. It is created on the host by signing in once —
# `docker compose run --rm ingest login`, which asks for the code on the console — and lives in
# a volume: a session file is a logged-in account, so it is never committed, never rsynced, and
# never copied between machines. The service refuses every sign-in prompt and reports that a
# person is needed, so until that has been run once, ingestion reads nothing.
#
# All three are required. The number is the account the session signs in as, in international
# form, and it is read by the login mode alone — the service is given no number on purpose,
# because handing one to the client library makes it ask the platform for a code, and sign-in
# attempts are a budget: enough of them locks the account out for most of a day.
TELEGRAM_API_ID=
TELEGRAM_API_HASH=
TELEGRAM_PHONE=

# Optional, with defaults in the code rather than in Compose. A malformed value stops the
# process naming the variable, rather than reading as unset.
#
# Not here: TELEGRAM_SESSION_DIRECTORY, where the client library keeps its own store *inside*
# the container. Compose sets it to the path the session volume is mounted at, because it is a
# property of the image rather than of the installation.
#
# This one, though, is a property of the machine, and it is read by Compose rather than by any
# service. Unset, the session lives in a volume of this project's own — which means one per
# worktree, and so one sign-in per worktree. Set to an absolute host path, every checkout shares
# one session and one sign-in serves all of them. Keep it OUTSIDE the repository: the production
# deploy rsyncs the tree, and a session file is a logged-in account. And run one ingest container
# at a time, because the client library locks its store.
# TELEGRAM_SESSION_HOST_PATH=/absolute/path/outside/the/repo
#
# The two conversations this build does not read, as chat identifiers: the assistant's own chat
# and the project coordination group. Both hold this system's own output rather than anyone's
# business, and ingesting them would let the assistant cite itself. Identifiers and not titles,
# because a title is renameable by anyone in the group and the exclusion that matters most is
# the one that must not lapse quietly.
# TELEGRAM_EXCLUDED_CHATS=
#
# The inverse, and empty almost always: naming conversations here reads those and nothing else.
# It exists for a bounded run — a handful of conversations read end to end — because the only
# other way to get one is to list everything you do not want, which nobody can keep correct. An
# exclusion still wins, so both can be set. A chat named here that the session does not list is
# reported at startup: a group's identifier is negative and a supergroup's begins -100, so an
# unconverted one is the likely mistake and it looks just like a quiet conversation.
# TELEGRAM_ONLY_CHATS=
#
# How long to leave between two history requests. The default is two seconds; raising it slows
# the backfill and lowers the chance of being asked to wait, which is the trade this knob is.
# TELEGRAM_SECONDS_BETWEEN_PAGES=2
#
# How far back to walk, as a source time. Unset reaches as far back as each conversation
# allows, which is what capture wants: fetching history is the expensive half, while indexing
# whatever comes back is local compute. This is depth and NOT the recency horizon — that one
# bounds what inference spends the model budget on, and the two answer to different costs.
# TELEGRAM_REACH_BACK_TO=2024-01-01T00:00:00Z

# --- the evaluation stack --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The evaluation set runs against a backend of its own, over a pinned fixture corpus rather than
# the real archive, and these are the settings that service overrides. Each one sets the pass
# setting of the same role inside that container — mostly the same name without the `EVAL_` prefix,
# though the two cadences below set the longer `*_SECONDS_BETWEEN_SWEEPS` names. The reason they
# exist at all is that the fixture corpus wants derivation finished in a minute, where the real one
# wants a cadence that does not re-read it all day.
#
# **Overriding is not the only way the two stacks differ, and this file cannot show the other way.**
# `backend-eval` is also handed *fewer* variables than the serving backend: the closure bounds, both
# models, both effort levels and the per-request ceiling are not passed to it at all, so the
# evaluation set scores the answer path on the code's own defaults whatever a deployment set. That
# is worth knowing before reading a score as though it described this deployment.
#
# **Every one has a default in `docker-compose.yml` and none has to be set.** They are here
# because a reader looking for why the evaluation stack behaves unlike the dev stack has nowhere
# else to find out: the eval harness's own file documents what the *suite* reads — its ports, its
# snapshot age, how long it waits for derivation — and these are read by Compose.
#
# How often the eval stack's two passes sweep, in seconds — against a minute for indexing and an
# hour for recognition on the dev stack. Both are short here because a run waits for derivation to
# finish before it scores anything, and a fixture corpus is one submission's worth of work.
# EVAL_INDEXING_SECONDS=5
# EVAL_RECOGNITION_SECONDS=5
#
# The recency horizon the eval stack recognises within, in days. Ten years, which is to say no
# horizon at all: the fixture's messages carry whatever source times the cases need, and a
# horizon sized for a live archive would silently put some of them out of reach and score the
# cases that depend on them as retrieval misses.
# EVAL_RECOGNITION_HORIZON_DAYS=3650
#
# Whether the eval stack infers. **True here and false for the dev stack** — every setting in this
# block disagrees with its counterpart, which is what makes them worth having, but this is the only
# one where the disagreement is a yes against a no rather than a figure against a figure. The
# evaluation set reads derived rows, so a run with inference off would score an answer path that
# had nothing to answer from and report it as a grounding collapse. This is also why a run costs
# what it costs.
# EVAL_RECOGNITION_INFERENCE_ENABLED=true
#
# What a day of eval inference may spend, in tokens. Far below the dev stack's allowance, and a
# backstop against a misconfigured run rather than what makes a run cheap — what bounds a run is
# the fixture corpus being finite, which `docker-compose.yml` states beside the same two variables.
# Note a reseed forgives the day's recorded spend here, so this bounds a rebuild rather than a day;
# tolerable for the same reason.
# EVAL_RECOGNITION_INPUT_TOKENS_PER_DAY=200000
# EVAL_RECOGNITION_OUTPUT_TOKENS_PER_DAY=50000
#
# Off here as it is everywhere. The fixture corpus names every conversation after the body of work
# it carries, so the pass would judge each one, reject none, and add its calls to what a scored run
# costs.
# EVAL_ADMISSION_ENABLED=false
#
# Where the eval stack publishes, on the loopback interface only. **These two are read twice, from
# different files, and that is why they are here as well as in the suite's own example.** Compose
# interpolates them to decide which host port to publish, and it reads `.env` and no other file;
# the eval harness reads the same names to decide where to connect, and it reads `eval/.env.local`
# first and then `.env`. So a port set only in the suite's file moves where the suite looks without
# moving where the stack listens, and the run fails to reach a stack that is up and well. Set it in
# `.env`, where both consumers see one value, or in both files with the same one — or export it for
# a one-off, which both also see.
# EVAL_POSTGRES_PORT=5433
# EVAL_BACKEND_PORT=18080
